1
00:00:01,000 --> 00:00:04,232
Before anesthesia, surgery was done on patients who

2
00:00:04,232 --> 00:00:05,245
were wide awake.

3
00:00:05,245 --> 00:00:08,224
A surgeon's best weapon against pain was speed.

4
00:00:08,674 --> 00:00:12,062
Then, on a Friday morning in October, 1846, in a

5
00:00:12,062 --> 00:00:15,732
Boston hospital, a dentist named William Morton gave

6
00:00:15,732 --> 00:00:18,062
a patient ether vapor to breathe.

7
00:00:18,511 --> 00:00:21,114
After about 5 minutes, the patient was asleep.

8
00:00:21,114 --> 00:00:23,547
The surgeon removed a growth from his neck.

9
00:00:23,547 --> 00:00:26,376
Afterward, the patient said it felt as if his neck

10
00:00:26,376 --> 00:00:27,338
had been scraped.

11
00:00:27,338 --> 00:00:28,413
But it hadn't hurt.

12
00:00:28,863 --> 00:00:31,575
The surgeon turned to the doctors watching, and,

13
00:00:31,575 --> 00:00:34,513
according to an account published years later, said:

14
00:00:34,813 --> 00:00:36,516
Gentlemen, this is no humbug.

15
00:00:37,316 --> 00:00:40,251
This is the story of how surgery stopped hurting.

16
00:00:40,251 --> 00:00:41,449
Why it took so long.

17
00:00:41,449 --> 00:00:43,066
Who fought over the credit.

18
00:00:43,066 --> 00:00:45,163
And how it reached the battlefield.

19
00:00:45,563 --> 00:00:46,299
Ether Day.

20
00:00:46,299 --> 00:00:48,657
The day surgery stopped hurting.

21
00:00:51,057 --> 00:00:54,191
To understand what changed, start with what surgery

22
00:00:54,191 --> 00:00:55,175
was like before.

23
00:00:56,175 --> 00:00:58,205
An operation was the last resort.

24
00:00:58,205 --> 00:01:01,281
Surgeons had to operate on conscious patients, who

25
00:01:01,281 --> 00:01:02,942
might be writhing in agony.

26
00:01:02,942 --> 00:01:04,973
And pain was not the only danger.

27
00:01:04,973 --> 00:01:07,741
Blood loss and infection made every operation

28
00:01:07,741 --> 00:01:08,787
life-threatening.

29
00:01:09,237 --> 00:01:13,128
Doctors had a few ways to dull the senses. Alcohol. Opium.

30
00:01:13,128 --> 00:01:15,945
In earlier centuries, plants like henbane.

31
00:01:15,945 --> 00:01:19,299
British sailors were given rum before an emergency

32
00:01:19,299 --> 00:01:20,037
amputation.

33
00:01:20,487 --> 00:01:21,948
None of it was reliable.

34
00:01:21,948 --> 00:01:24,444
So surgeons worked as fast as they could.

35
00:01:24,944 --> 00:01:28,092
In London, the surgeon Robert Liston was famous for

36
00:01:28,092 --> 00:01:30,931
the speed with which he could amputate a limb.

37
00:01:30,931 --> 00:01:34,017
Even a busy surgeon like him performed only two or

38
00:01:34,017 --> 00:01:35,560
three operations a month.

39
00:01:36,010 --> 00:01:40,742
Between 1835 and 1840, he performed 66 amputations.

40
00:01:40,742 --> 00:01:43,154
10 of those patients died.

41
00:01:43,604 --> 00:01:46,591
You may have heard that one of his operations killed

42
00:01:46,591 --> 00:01:47,797
three people at once.

43
00:01:47,797 --> 00:01:49,979
Historians call that story apocryphal.

44
00:01:49,979 --> 00:01:52,162
There is no good evidence it happened.

45
00:01:52,662 --> 00:01:54,837
Patients left their own accounts.

46
00:01:54,837 --> 00:01:58,264
In 1811, an English writer had an operation in Paris

47
00:01:58,264 --> 00:02:00,835
with only a wine cordial to steady her.

48
00:02:01,285 --> 00:02:02,780
She remembered how it began.

49
00:02:02,780 --> 00:02:05,502
Her room was entered, without warning, by seven men

50
00:02:05,502 --> 00:02:05,982
in black.

51
00:02:05,982 --> 00:02:08,597
And she remembered the glitter of polished steel.

52
00:02:09,047 --> 00:02:12,061
Months later, she wrote to her sister about it.

53
00:02:12,061 --> 00:02:15,523
She called it a terror that surpasses all description.

54
00:02:15,523 --> 00:02:16,421
And she added:

55
00:02:16,421 --> 00:02:18,217
I dare not revise, nor read.

56
00:02:18,217 --> 00:02:20,589
The recollection is still so painful.

57
00:02:21,389 --> 00:02:23,373
And yet, a clue had been there for decades.

58
00:02:24,373 --> 00:02:27,946
In 1800, the English chemist Humphry Davy wrote that

59
00:02:27,946 --> 00:02:30,970
nitrous oxide appeared capable of destroying

60
00:02:30,970 --> 00:02:31,932
physical pain.

61
00:02:31,932 --> 00:02:35,848
He suggested it might be used during surgical operations.

62
00:02:36,298 --> 00:02:38,719
For decades, almost no one followed up.

63
00:02:38,719 --> 00:02:41,388
Instead, nitrous oxide became laughing gas.

64
00:02:41,388 --> 00:02:44,367
At shows and parties, people passed it around in

65
00:02:44,367 --> 00:02:46,353
bladders and inhaled it for fun.

66
00:02:46,803 --> 00:02:47,813
Why the delay?

67
00:02:47,813 --> 00:02:49,761
Historians still debate it.

68
00:02:49,761 --> 00:02:51,565
But the result was clear.

69
00:02:51,565 --> 00:02:54,884
For nearly half a century, the implications of

70
00:02:54,884 --> 00:02:57,842
Davy's findings for surgery were ignored.

71
00:02:58,242 --> 00:03:01,617
In Georgia, on March 30, 1842, a country doctor

72
00:03:01,617 --> 00:03:04,848
named Crawford Long used ether while removing

73
00:03:04,848 --> 00:03:07,002
growths from a patient's neck.

74
00:03:07,452 --> 00:03:11,106
But Long didn't publish his results until 1849.

75
00:03:11,106 --> 00:03:14,759
Today, America's National Doctors' Day falls on

76
00:03:14,759 --> 00:03:17,636
March 30, in honor of that operation.

77
00:03:17,636 --> 00:03:21,445
It was first observed in Georgia in 1933, and was

78
00:03:21,445 --> 00:03:24,321
first recognized by Congress in 1990.

79
00:03:24,821 --> 00:03:27,733
In December 1844, a dentist in Hartford,

80
00:03:27,733 --> 00:03:31,372
Connecticut, named Horace Wells went to a laughing

81
00:03:31,372 --> 00:03:32,028
gas show.

82
00:03:32,478 --> 00:03:35,568
The next day, he breathed the gas himself, while a

83
00:03:35,568 --> 00:03:37,669
colleague pulled one of his teeth.

84
00:03:37,669 --> 00:03:38,658
He felt no pain.

85
00:03:39,108 --> 00:03:42,430
In January 1845, Wells tried a demonstration in

86
00:03:42,430 --> 00:03:46,246
Boston, arranged with the surgeon John Collins Warren.

87
00:03:46,246 --> 00:03:47,801
The patient cried out.

88
00:03:47,801 --> 00:03:51,264
The demonstration was judged a failure. A humbug.

89
00:03:51,764 --> 00:03:54,957
One man who knew Wells well was his former partner.

90
00:03:54,957 --> 00:03:56,897
A dentist named William Morton.

91
00:03:57,497 --> 00:03:59,509
Remember those names.

92
00:03:59,509 --> 00:04:00,850
Davy, in 1800.

93
00:04:00,850 --> 00:04:02,192
Long, in 1842.

94
00:04:02,192 --> 00:04:03,629
Wells, in 1844.

95
00:04:03,629 --> 00:04:05,162
Each came close.

96
00:04:05,162 --> 00:04:10,144
Each, for different reasons, did not change surgery.

97
00:04:10,144 --> 00:04:11,964
The next man would.

98
00:04:12,564 --> 00:04:15,231
Morton turned to a different chemical. Ether.

99
00:04:16,231 --> 00:04:19,877
On Friday, October 16, 1846, Morton was invited to

100
00:04:19,877 --> 00:04:22,940
an operation at ten in the morning, in the

101
00:04:22,940 --> 00:04:25,419
hospital's operating amphitheater.

102
00:04:25,419 --> 00:04:28,044
The surgeon was John Collins Warren.

103
00:04:28,494 --> 00:04:31,527
Morton went to the patient, took his hand, and told

104
00:04:31,527 --> 00:04:33,073
him he would feel no pain.

105
00:04:33,523 --> 00:04:35,322
The patient breathed in the vapor.

106
00:04:35,322 --> 00:04:37,121
In about 5 minutes, he was asleep.

107
00:04:37,121 --> 00:04:39,079
Warren operated to remove the growth.

108
00:04:39,529 --> 00:04:42,276
When it was over, the patient said he had felt his

109
00:04:42,276 --> 00:04:43,319
neck being scraped.

110
00:04:43,319 --> 00:04:44,363
But it hadn't hurt.

111
00:04:44,813 --> 00:04:47,370
Warren, the story goes, turned to the room.

112
00:04:47,370 --> 00:04:49,094
Gentlemen, this is no humbug.

113
00:04:49,094 --> 00:04:51,353
Some historians doubt he ever said it.

114
00:04:51,353 --> 00:04:53,671
But the room had seen what it had seen.

115
00:04:54,471 --> 00:04:56,111
The room still exists.

116
00:04:56,111 --> 00:04:59,763
It was the hospital's operating amphitheater from

117
00:04:59,763 --> 00:05:00,732
1821 to 1867.

118
00:05:00,732 --> 00:05:03,191
Today it's called the Ether Dome.

119
00:05:03,641 --> 00:05:06,601
It sits on the fourth floor of the hospital's

120
00:05:06,601 --> 00:05:07,784
Bulfinch Building.

121
00:05:07,784 --> 00:05:11,073
It was named a National Historic Landmark in 1965,

122
00:05:11,073 --> 00:05:14,098
and it's still used for meetings and lectures.

123
00:05:14,698 --> 00:05:16,014
The news spread fast.

124
00:05:16,014 --> 00:05:17,582
And so did the arguments.

125
00:05:18,582 --> 00:05:22,099
On November 18, 1846, the surgeon Henry Jacob

126
00:05:22,099 --> 00:05:25,694
Bigelow published an account of Morton's work.

127
00:05:26,144 --> 00:05:29,598
A few days later, the physician and writer Oliver

128
00:05:29,598 --> 00:05:33,123
Wendell Holmes suggested, in a letter to Morton, a

129
00:05:33,123 --> 00:05:35,660
word for the new state. Anaesthesia.

130
00:05:35,660 --> 00:05:38,762
And an adjective to go with it: anaesthetic.

131
00:05:39,212 --> 00:05:41,259
Morton had tried to keep his secret.

132
00:05:41,259 --> 00:05:43,704
At first, he would not even reveal what his

133
00:05:43,704 --> 00:05:44,500
substance was.

134
00:05:44,500 --> 00:05:47,684
He called it Letheon, and he was granted a patent on it.

135
00:05:47,684 --> 00:05:50,583
The patent was later revoked, and he never received

136
00:05:50,583 --> 00:05:51,379
any royalties.

137
00:05:51,829 --> 00:05:53,997
The news crossed the Atlantic.

138
00:05:53,997 --> 00:05:57,756
On December 21, 1846, at University College Hospital

139
00:05:57,756 --> 00:06:00,935
in London, Robert Liston performed the first

140
00:06:00,935 --> 00:06:04,332
recorded major operation under ether in Europe.

141
00:06:04,782 --> 00:06:07,917
Liston, the fastest knife in London, asked the

142
00:06:07,917 --> 00:06:11,393
audience to time him, even with his patient asleep.

143
00:06:11,393 --> 00:06:14,051
Afterward, he reportedly told the room:

144
00:06:14,051 --> 00:06:16,845
This Yankee dodge beats mesmerism hollow.

145
00:06:17,295 --> 00:06:20,725
A year later, in Scotland, the obstetrician James

146
00:06:20,725 --> 00:06:24,715
Young Simpson began using another anesthetic. Chloroform.

147
00:06:25,215 --> 00:06:27,900
Back in America, a bitter fight began over who

148
00:06:27,900 --> 00:06:29,535
deserved the credit. Morton.

149
00:06:29,535 --> 00:06:31,461
His former partner, Horace Wells.

150
00:06:31,461 --> 00:06:34,731
The chemist Charles Jackson, who shared Morton's patent.

151
00:06:34,731 --> 00:06:36,482
And Crawford Long, in Georgia.

152
00:06:36,932 --> 00:06:40,114
Boston physicians petitioned Congress to reward the

153
00:06:40,114 --> 00:06:40,800
discoverer.

154
00:06:40,800 --> 00:06:42,983
Bills were introduced. None passed.

155
00:06:42,983 --> 00:06:45,416
The dispute was never clearly resolved.

156
00:06:45,866 --> 00:06:50,487
Morton spent his last years in controversy and lawsuits.

157
00:06:50,487 --> 00:06:53,293
He died in 1868, at the age of 48.

158
00:06:54,093 --> 00:06:56,757
Anesthesia didn't stay in the hospital for long.

159
00:06:57,757 --> 00:07:01,289
Within six months of Ether Day, it had gone to war.

160
00:07:01,289 --> 00:07:04,753
In March 1847, during the Mexican-American War, an

161
00:07:04,753 --> 00:07:08,285
American surgeon named Edward Barton gave ether for

162
00:07:08,285 --> 00:07:10,086
an amputation at Veracruz.

163
00:07:10,536 --> 00:07:12,446
14 years later came the Civil War.

164
00:07:12,446 --> 00:07:14,861
And with it, a myth that lasts to this day.

165
00:07:14,861 --> 00:07:17,669
That wounded soldiers bit a bullet, or were simply

166
00:07:17,669 --> 00:07:20,253
given alcohol, because there was nothing else.

167
00:07:20,703 --> 00:07:23,964
The Union army's own medical records tell a

168
00:07:23,964 --> 00:07:25,177
different story.

169
00:07:25,177 --> 00:07:29,196
At least 80,000 operations were done with anesthesia.

170
00:07:29,196 --> 00:07:31,775
Only 254 were recorded without it.

171
00:07:32,225 --> 00:07:35,341
In the field, chloroform was used most often.

172
00:07:35,341 --> 00:07:37,349
Ether, in about 14% of cases.

173
00:07:37,349 --> 00:07:40,672
And William Morton himself served in Union field

174
00:07:40,672 --> 00:07:43,788
hospitals, as one of the war's only dedicated

175
00:07:43,788 --> 00:07:44,688
anesthetists.

176
00:07:45,188 --> 00:07:48,182
The World Wars made one specialist essential.

177
00:07:48,182 --> 00:07:49,646
The nurse anesthetist.

178
00:07:49,646 --> 00:07:52,906
In World War One, nurse anesthetist Sophie Winton

179
00:07:52,906 --> 00:07:55,833
served with Mobile Hospital No. 1 in France.

180
00:07:56,283 --> 00:08:00,543
In World War Two, the Army estimated it needed 2,495

181
00:08:00,543 --> 00:08:02,099
nurse anesthetists.

182
00:08:02,099 --> 00:08:03,901
Only 273 were on duty.

183
00:08:03,901 --> 00:08:07,669
More than 2,000 would have to be recruited, or

184
00:08:07,669 --> 00:08:10,209
trained in new 6-month courses.

185
00:08:10,659 --> 00:08:13,647
One Army nurse anesthetist, Mildred Clark, was at

186
00:08:13,647 --> 00:08:16,636
Schofield Barracks in Hawaii during the attack on

187
00:08:16,636 --> 00:08:17,429
Pearl Harbor.

188
00:08:17,429 --> 00:08:20,479
She later became the first nurse anesthetist to be

189
00:08:20,479 --> 00:08:22,918
appointed Chief of the Army Nurse Corps.

190
00:08:23,368 --> 00:08:26,762
Today, the Army trains its own nurse anesthetists,

191
00:08:26,762 --> 00:08:27,508
in the U.S.

192
00:08:27,508 --> 00:08:31,445
Army Graduate Program in Anesthesia Nursing. In 2011, U.S.

193
00:08:31,445 --> 00:08:34,567
News and World Report ranked it the best nurse

194
00:08:34,567 --> 00:08:36,874
anesthesia program in the country.

195
00:08:37,324 --> 00:08:41,269
Military anesthesiologists work in combat hospitals.

196
00:08:41,269 --> 00:08:44,834
This team was at the NATO hospital in Kandahar,

197
00:08:44,834 --> 00:08:46,427
Afghanistan, in 2015.

198
00:08:46,877 --> 00:08:50,121
And in 2020, aboard the Navy hospital ship Comfort

199
00:08:50,121 --> 00:08:52,976
in New York, a recovery unit stood ready for

200
00:08:52,976 --> 00:08:55,117
patients waking up after surgery.

201
00:08:55,717 --> 00:08:58,301
So what does anesthesia actually do today?

202
00:08:59,301 --> 00:09:02,048
According to the National Library of Medicine,

203
00:09:02,048 --> 00:09:05,093
general anesthesia puts you into a deep, sleep-like

204
00:09:05,093 --> 00:09:08,079
state, so you do not feel pain during surgery, and

205
00:09:08,079 --> 00:09:10,050
you don't remember the procedure.

206
00:09:10,500 --> 00:09:11,924
It's not the only kind.

207
00:09:11,924 --> 00:09:15,018
Local anesthesia numbs a small area, like a tooth.

208
00:09:15,018 --> 00:09:18,237
Regional anesthesia numbs a larger area, like an arm

209
00:09:18,237 --> 00:09:18,794
or a leg.

210
00:09:18,794 --> 00:09:21,765
And sedation makes a patient relaxed, or sleepy.

211
00:09:22,215 --> 00:09:25,511
During general anesthesia, blood pressure, pulse and

212
00:09:25,511 --> 00:09:27,857
breathing are monitored continuously.

213
00:09:27,857 --> 00:09:30,836
Most often, the anesthesia is given by a doctor

214
00:09:30,836 --> 00:09:32,548
called an anesthesiologist.

215
00:09:32,548 --> 00:09:34,703
Sometimes, by a nurse anesthetist.

216
00:09:35,153 --> 00:09:36,467
Training never stops.

217
00:09:36,467 --> 00:09:39,721
Operating teams rehearse and review together, so the

218
00:09:39,721 --> 00:09:40,972
real day goes right.

219
00:09:41,422 --> 00:09:44,798
The American Society of Anesthesiologists says

220
00:09:44,798 --> 00:09:48,908
surgery and anesthesia are safer today than ever before.

221
00:09:49,508 --> 00:09:53,206
Before 1846, a surgeon raced the patient's pain, and

222
00:09:53,206 --> 00:09:55,979
the patient was awake for every second.

223
00:09:56,429 --> 00:10:00,238
After one Friday morning in Boston, patients could sleep.

224
00:10:00,238 --> 00:10:03,445
And today, surgical teams like this one can take

225
00:10:03,445 --> 00:10:04,180
their time.

226
00:10:04,630 --> 00:10:07,661
Within a year, the news had crossed an ocean.

227
00:10:07,661 --> 00:10:11,096
Within 15 years, it was in field hospitals across a

228
00:10:11,096 --> 00:10:12,039
nation at war.

229
00:10:12,039 --> 00:10:15,070
And it began with a dentist, a surgeon, and a

230
00:10:15,070 --> 00:10:17,225
patient who said it didn't hurt.

231
00:10:17,825 --> 00:10:21,582
Every year on October 16, the anniversary of Ether

232
00:10:21,582 --> 00:10:25,339
Day, anesthesiologists around the world mark World

233
00:10:25,339 --> 00:10:26,541
Anaesthesia Day.

234
00:10:26,541 --> 00:10:29,472
This October was the 180th anniversary.

235
00:10:30,372 --> 00:10:32,737
If this story surprised you, subscribe.

236
00:10:32,737 --> 00:10:34,921
Next time on KEPT ALIVE: penicillin.

237
00:10:34,921 --> 00:10:36,498
The mold that went to war.

238
00:10:37,298 --> 00:10:38,438
For education only.

239
00:10:38,438 --> 00:10:40,059
This is not medical advice.

240
00:10:40,059 --> 00:10:42,880
If you have a health concern, talk to a doctor.
